Everything else under `mulan/` and `scripts/` is **original to this fork** and was never part of
upstream MuLAN -- roughly 2,900 lines, chiefly `mulan/foldx/`, `mulan/plm/`, `mulan/metrics/`,
`mulan/splits/`, `mulan/interface_xattn.py` and `scripts/audit_local_results.py`. Crediting those
to the upstream authors would misattribute work they have not seen.

`LICENSE` reproduces upstream's copyright line with one correction: upstream spells the first
author "Ginluca"; this repository spells it "Gianluca".

The substantive additions in this fork are:

  1. **Multiple PLM embedding backends.** Upstream already ships a seven-entry HuggingFace
     registry (`PLM_ENCODERS`: ESM-2 3B/650M/35M, Ankh large/base, ProtBERT, ProtT5-XL-half)
     with `get_available_plms()` and family-branching load logic. This fork consolidates those
     surfaces behind one registry and extends it to SDK-only and offline-embedding sources
     (ProstT5, SaProt, ESM-C, ESM3, AIDO, MINT), adding per-model tokenization prefixes, strip
     rules, dtype and device policy.

  2. **A FoldX augmentation channel.** An encoder-independent zero-shot score channel
     carrying FoldX binding ΔΔG, in two arms: a single Interaction-Energy scalar and a
     12-term decomposed vector consumed by a small MLP. What this repository contains is
     the *consuming* half — the mergers that join scores onto evaluation splits
     (`mulan/foldx/merge.py`) and a vendored snapshot of the computed results
     (`data/foldx/`). The *producing* half — the compute pipeline
     (RepairPDB → BuildModel → AnalyseComplex), the result store and its reader API —
     was extracted to skempi-foldx and is a dependency of this package, not part of it.

  3. **Leakage-controlled evaluation.** The upstream random 10-fold cross-validation was
     supplemented with by-complex, sequence-clustered (mmseqs ≤60%) and CATH-superfamily
     hold-out protocols, evaluated with per-structure Spearman correlation and AUROC so the
     results are directly comparable with the published ΔΔG frontier.

  4. **Additional model heads**, including interface cross-attention, a pooled wild-type
     structural-context branch, and a structure gate over 3Di embeddings.

  5. **Apple Silicon / MPS support**, cross-platform device selection, and an in-memory
     embedding cache.

Neither Gianluca Lombardi nor Alessandra Carbone has reviewed or endorsed this fork, and
they are not responsible for it. Any errors introduced here are the fork author's.


Third-party data and assets
---------------------------

Neither upstream MuLAN nor this fork produced the underlying measurements. Everything below is
someone else's work that this repository depends on, redistributes, or derives from.

  * **SKEMPI 2.0** — the source of every ΔΔG label used here, and of the chain groupings and
    cleaned mutation strings the FoldX pipeline runs on. **Any table in this repository carrying
    SKEMPI ΔΔG values or SKEMPI-derived mutation strings is an adaptation of it** -- not only the
    evaluation splits under `data/splits/`, but the row sets and fold files under
    `experiments/`, `experiments/embedding_sweep/data/wt_sequences.fasta` (sequences extracted
    from SKEMPI's cleaned PDBs), and `examples/S1102.tsv`. The SKEMPI database itself is not
    redistributed.

    SKEMPI 2.0 is released under **CC BY 4.0**
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits these adaptations and requires
    that the licence be identified and the modifications indicated -- which this paragraph does.
    `examples/S1102.tsv` is a further adaptation: it is Lombardi & Carbone's curation of SKEMPI
    rows, redistributed here verbatim from upstream MuLAN.

        Jankauskaite, J., Jimenez-Garcia, B., Dapkunas, J., Fernandez-Recio, J. & Moal, I. H.
        "SKEMPI 2.0: an updated benchmark of changes in protein-protein binding energy,
        kinetics and thermodynamics upon mutation."
        Bioinformatics 35(3):462-469, 2019.  doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/bty635

        https://life.bsc.es/pid/skempi2

  * **The S4169 benchmark selection** determines which mutations 2,494 of the 4,238 shipped
    single-point FoldX records cover. The ddG values and the `cleaned` mutation strings are
    SKEMPI's, but the per-complex *lists* passed to BuildModel for those records came from S4169.
    Each record names this in its `_source` field, and `data/foldx/CONSOLIDATION_REPORT.txt`
    reports the split by source.

    This is load-bearing rather than bibliographic: as `OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` establishes, a
    mutation's FoldX ddG depends on every entry before it in the list it was computed with, so
    the row selection materially set those values. It is therefore worth being exact about whose
    selection it is.

    **S4169 is mCSM-PPI2's, not GeoPPI's**, though it was obtained here from GeoPPI's copy.
    GeoPPI's own paper attributes it: "Rodrigues et al. filtered single-point mutations and
    selected 4,169 variants from 319 different complexes, called the S4169 dataset." The two
    files agree row for row -- 4,169 of 4,169 PDB identifiers and 4,169 of 4,169 ddG values,
    row-aligned -- so GeoPPI's contribution to the artifact is a residue renumbering and a
    column rename, which is what this repository consumed and what its `_source` labels record.
    S1131 likewise predates GeoPPI: it is Xiong et al.'s non-redundant interface set, used the
    same way for the benchmark tables.

    mCSM-PPI2's article is CC BY 4.0 and its dataset page is marked Open Knowledge; GeoPPI is
    MIT (https://github.com/Liuxg16/GeoPPI, LICENSE at root). Attribution for the selection runs
    to mCSM-PPI2, and for the values to SKEMPI 2.0.

        Rodrigues, C. H. M., Myung, Y., Pires, D. E. V. & Ascher, D. B. "mCSM-PPI2: predicting
        the effects of mutations on protein-protein interactions."
        Nucleic Acids Research 47(W1):W338-W344, 2019.  doi:10.1093/nar/gkz383

        Xiong, P., Zhang, C., Zheng, W. & Zhang, Y. "BindProfX: Assessing Mutation-Induced
        Binding Affinity Change by Protein Interface Profiles with Pseudo-Counts."
        Journal of Molecular Biology 429(3):426-434, 2017.  doi:10.1016/j.jmb.2016.11.022

        Liu, X., Luo, Y., Li, P., Song, S. & Peng, J. "Deep geometric representations for
        modeling effects of mutations on protein-protein binding affinity."
        PLOS Computational Biology 17(8):e1009284, 2021.  doi:10.1371/journal.pcbi.1009284
        (the copy these lists were taken from)

  * **The CATH-superfamily partition is not ours, and is NOT redistributed here.** It is a join
    of our rows onto the static `cath_fold` column that USP-ddG ships in its own
    `data/SKEMPI2/skempi_v2.csv` (https://github.com/ak422/USP-ddG), following the
    CATH-superfamily hold-out protocol introduced by CATH-ddG. We reuse it deliberately, so that
    a number produced here is placeable directly in their tables. Credit for the partition
    belongs to those authors.

    USP-ddG's repository shows an MIT badge in its README but ships no LICENSE file; its terms are
    stated here as found rather than asserted. Which of the two papers first constructed the
    partition cannot be established from either repository -- USP-ddG's code regenerates labels
    from inputs it does not ship -- so both are cited and neither is claimed as sole originator.

    Because those terms cannot be established, `data/splits/splits_skempi_full_cath_kfold/`
    carries only a build recipe and a checksum manifest. Obtain the split by cloning USP-ddG and
    running `experiments/full_skempi_seqonly/build_split_cath.py --usp <their csv>`; the output is
    byte-reproducible and `MANIFEST.sha256` confirms it is the split our numbers were computed on.
    For the same reason the CATH tier's predictions under `results/` ship unkeyed -- keyed rows
    would disclose the partition, since our test rows are the complement of train and val within
    the rows this tier keeps.
    That directory's README documents both. Everything else there -- the row curation, the ddG
    values from SKEMPI 2.0, the seeded val carve, the model outputs -- is ours or SKEMPI's and is
    published in full.

        Yu, G., Bi, X., Ma, T., Li, Y. & Wang, J. "CATH-ddG: towards robust mutation effect
        prediction on protein-protein interactions out of CATH homologous superfamily."
        Bioinformatics 41(Supplement 1):i362-i372, 2025.  doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btaf228

        Yu, G., Bi, X., Zhao, Q. & Wang, J. "USP-ddG: a unified structural paradigm with data
        efficacy and mixture-of-experts for predicting mutational effects on protein-protein
        interactions."  Bioinformatics 42(Supplement 1):btag249, 2026.
        doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btag249  (preprinted as doi:10.1101/2025.11.09.687124)

    The underlying CATH superfamily classification is maintained by the CATH/Gene3D group
    (https://www.cathdb.info/) and is distributed under CC BY 4.0, which carries a citation
    requirement:

        Sillitoe, I., Bordin, N., Dawson, N. et al. "CATH: increased structural coverage of
        functional space."  Nucleic Acids Research 49(D1):D266-D273, 2021.
        doi:10.1093/nar/gkaa1079

  * **SIFTS** (EMBL-EBI) supplies the PDB-chain to CATH-superfamily mapping that
    `experiments/cath_leakage/cath_families.json` is derived from, via
    `pdb_chain_cath_uniprot.csv`. That file records its own source in its `meta.sifts_source`
    field. SIFTS is distributed by EMBL-EBI under CC BY 4.0.

        Dana, J. M. et al. "SIFTS: updated Structure Integration with Function, Taxonomy and
        Sequences resource allows 40-fold increase in coverage of structure-based annotations
        for proteins."  Nucleic Acids Research 47(D1):D482-D489, 2019.
        doi:10.1093/nar/gky1114

        https://www.ebi.ac.uk/pdbe/docs/sifts/

  * **Protein Data Bank structures.** The FoldX channel is computed on SKEMPI's cleaned PDB
    files. Structures are not redistributed here; the computed FoldX *outputs* are.

  * **AlphaFold Protein Structure Database** (EMBL-EBI / Google DeepMind) supplies the predicted
    structures the per-residue structural context is computed from. `scripts/uniprot_visualize.py`
    fetches models from it at run time, and the 3Di tokens described in
    `mulan/EMBEDDING_PREPROCESSING.md` are computed from the AlphaFold backbone. Structures are
    not redistributed here, but values *derived* from them are: the RSA and contact fields in
    `experiments/structctx_sweep/results/*.json`, computed under the pLDDT mask each file names.
    AFDB is distributed under CC BY 4.0, which carries a citation requirement, and asks that the
    method paper and the database paper be cited separately:

        Jumper, J., Evans, R., Pritzel, A. et al. "Highly accurate protein structure prediction
        with AlphaFold."  Nature 596:583-589, 2021.  doi:10.1038/s41586-021-03819-2

        Bertoni, D., Tsenkov, M., Magana, P. et al. "AlphaFold Protein Structure Database 2025:
        a redesigned interface and updated structural coverage."  Nucleic Acids Research
        54(D1):D358-D362, 2025.  doi:10.1093/nar/gkaf1226
        (supersedes the 2024 database paper, doi:10.1093/nar/gkad1011)

    The RSA and secondary-structure values in those files are produced by DSSP, cited under
    `references` in CITATION.cff. First three authors plus the senior author in both entries
    above; see each paper for the full list.

  * **MMseqs2** (Steinegger & Söding, Nature Biotechnology 35:1026-1028, 2017) was used to build
    the <=60% sequence-identity clustered splits.

  * **"MuLAN attention scores for PDB chains"** (Lombardi, G., Zenodo record 18175031,
    2025-12-18, CC BY 4.0, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18175031) -- upstream's released
    Ankh-large attention and the interface labels it is scored against -- is the input to
    `experiments/attention_interface/score_interactome.py` and to every measured number in
    `experiments/attention_interface/RESULTS_interactome.md` (its `paper` column is transcribed
    from the upstream publication). The record itself is not
    redistributed; the scores derived from it are.

  * **reveal.js**, vendored under `scripts_plots/artifacts/*/deck*/vendor/` for the
    self-contained result decks. MIT licensed, Copyright (c) 2011-2023 Hakim El Hattab
    (https://revealjs.com). The vendored files are not all one release -- `reveal.js` is 4.6.0
    and `reveal.css` is 4.5.0 -- and the MIT header is retained in `reveal.js` and `reveal.css`
    but not in the vendored theme `white.css`, which carries only its author line. The MIT
    license is compatible with, and independent of, this repository's CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 terms:
    reveal.js remains MIT for anyone who extracts it.

Comparator numbers quoted in `docs/` and `experiments/BENCHMARK_MATRIX.md` are the respective
authors' published results, cited there and reproduced only for comparison. The set the figures
draw from is `data/benchmarks/frontier.tsv`, one row per (method, metric, protocol) with the
source paper and table named per row; it holds transcribed third-party results only, and never
any number measured in this repository.


Related projects
----------------

Two modules developed inside this codebase were extracted into standalone projects and are
maintained separately. Both are dependencies of this package rather than parts of it, and each
carries its own terms — which are not the same as each other's. Consult the licence in the
project itself; the summaries below are orientation, not a grant.

    skempi-foldx — https://github.com/cchin29/skempi-foldx
        The FoldX compute pipeline, per-complex result store and reader API. MIT for the
        code, CC BY 4.0 for the SKEMPI-derived energies. A required dependency, pinned to
        v0.1.0 in pyproject.toml. Neither term is this repository's, so code obtained from
        skempi-foldx is not encumbered by the non-commercial condition below.

    foldenv — https://github.com/cchin29/foldenv
        Per-residue structural context (RSA, secondary structure, contacts). Reached here
        through the optional `struct` extra. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 — the same terms as this
        repository, because foldenv adapts portions of MuLAN itself. The non-commercial
        and share-alike conditions travel with it.


